<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Mauretania Tingitana</h1><p class="st-meta">42 CE – 429 CE (approx.) · capital Tingis</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Roman province of northern Morocco, ruled from Tangier and later attached to Roman Spain.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the execution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-of-mauretania">King Ptolemy</a> and the suppression of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aedemon">Aedemon's</a> revolt, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> divided the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-mauretania">Kingdom of Mauretania</a> in 42 CE. The western part became Mauretania Tingitana, named after its capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tingis</a> (Tangier), and was governed by an equestrian procurator.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Roman control covered the north of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-morocco">Morocco</a>, with towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volubilis">Volubilis</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chellah">Sala</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lixus">Lixus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banasa">Banasa</a> linked by roads and protected by forts. The economy produced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a>, grain, fish sauce and wild animals for the arena. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> tribes such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baquates">Baquates</a> negotiated treaties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, recorded on altars at Volubilis. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> the province was attached to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocese-of-hispania">Diocese of the Spains</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 285 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> withdrew from the south, keeping only the region around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tingis</a> and the coast. In 429 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> crossed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, and Roman rule ended; the strongpoint of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/septem">Septem</a> later became a Byzantine outpost, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> principalities rose inland.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/mauretania-tingitana.jpg" alt="Mauretania Tingitana" width="320" height="267"><figcaption>Image: Milenioscuro · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_Empire_-_Mauretania_Tingitana_(125_AD).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>42 CE – 429 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> – <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tingis</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman religion, Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin, Berber</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/morocco">Morocco</a>, <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tingitana</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=236&amp;focus=mauretania-tingitana" aria-label="Mauretania Tingitana on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4534 886 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Mauretania Tingitana lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4854 1142L4830 1267M4854 1142L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4830 1267h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4854 1142h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4854 1142h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=236&amp;focus=mauretania-tingitana">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
